## Object Methods
Objects can have functions as properties. These are called methods:

```ts
const channel = {
    name: "Steve's Channel",
    subs: 3,
    greet() {
        console.log("Welcome to " + this.name);
    }
};
channel.greet();  // Welcome to Steve's Channel
```

`this` refers to the object the method belongs to. Inside `greet`, `this.name` is `channel.name`.

### Warning:
`this` in TypeScript/JavaScript is confusing. It depends on HOW the function is called, not where it's defined:
```ts
const greet = channel.greet;
greet();  // 'this' is undefined! Not channel anymore
```

Arrow functions don't have their own `this` - they inherit from the surrounding scope.

